お仕事ってそんなめちゃくちゃ早いの？通勤とかじゃないの？9時開始とかじゃないの？フレックスってやつ？
Is work really that early? Don't you commute? Doesn't it start at 9:00? Is it that flextime thing?

197
00:22:09,532 --> 00:22:10,532
早い。
That's early.

198
00:22:14,032 --> 00:22:18,032
Did you train a lot in the Peak stream?

199
00:22:18,032 --> 00:22:22,032
English? Peak in Peak stream? だったらYesかも。
English? Peak in the Peak stream? If so, then maybe yes.

200
00:22:22,532 --> 00:22:26,532
英語…英語ね、勉強したよ。
English... I studied English!

201
00:22:26,532 --> 00:22:35,032
I studied Peak. Peak 勉強したよ。
I studied Peak. I studied Peak.

202
00:22:35,632 --> 00:22:44,232
7時半開始？7時半には職場？早くない？朝部活行く時間じゃよ、それ。早い！
Starting at 7:30? At work by 7:30? Isn't that early? That's when students go to morning club practice. That's so early!

203
00:22:44,632 --> 00:22:49,032
今から帰る？夜勤なのかな？フレックスなら可能？へー。
Going home now? Was it a night shift? Possible with flextime? Huh.

204
00:22:51,032 --> 00:22:56,032
Peak the universal language? Peak?

205
00:22:56,532 --> 00:23:03,232
確かに朝配信…配信者の人って夜配信するから朝起きれないんじゃよね。
Indeed, morning streams... Streamers stream at night so they can't wake up in the morning, right?

206
00:23:03,532 --> 00:23:13,032
サマータイム、うわ、あったー！サマータイムとかあった！なんか1時間ずれるやつじゃろ？時計1時間ずらせやつじゃろ？なんかあった気するな、そんなの。あんま覚えてないの。
Daylight saving time... Whoa, right! There was daylight saving time! That thing where it shifts by one hour, right? Shifting the clock by an hour, right? I feel like that existed. I don't really remember it though.

207
00:23:16,432 --> 00:23:20,932
水曜日だけ7時に会社にいるようにしてる？わしのために？サンキューのじゃ。
You make sure to be at work at 7:00 only on Wednesdays? For my sake? Thank you, noja.

208
00:23:23,932 --> 00:23:25,232
Okay.

209
00:23:26,732 --> 00:23:36,532
朝強い？え、弱くはないよ。眠そうだと思うけど、なんか起きれはするんじゃよ、眠くてもね。眠くてもね。
Am I good with mornings? Well, I'm not bad. You might think I look sleepy, but I can wake up even if I'm tired. Even if I'm tired.

210
00:23:37,732 --> 00:23:47,832
メラって何歳？415歳。I am 415 years old, noja.
How old am I? 415 years old. I am 415 years old, noja.

211
00:23:48,832 --> 00:23:55,232
弱くはない。だって起きれるもん。予定があったら起きれはするのじゃ。起きれるだけね。
I'm not bad at mornings. Because I can wake up. If I have plans, I can manage to wake up, noja. Just waking up, though.

212
00:23:55,632 --> 00:23:57,632
朝ご飯食べてから配信してるの？
Did I eat breakfast before streaming?

213
00:23:57,832 --> 00:24:04,232
I ate Pokémon bread today. Pokémon. Pokémon bread.

214
00:24:04,632 --> 00:24:18,232
母が買ってくれたやつ。母がなんかスーパーで買わ…買ってきてくれたポケモンパンを食べたよ。あの、チョコ蒸しパンみたいな。蒸しパンみたいなやつ。
The one my mom bought me. I ate the Pokémon bread my mom bought at the supermarket. That chocolate steamed bread-like... steamed bun-like one.

215
00:24:19,032 --> 00:24:21,032
ゼリー冷やすの忘れてた。
I forgot to chill my jelly.

216
00:24:21,532 --> 00:24:28,232
ゼリーはね、今日冷やすの忘れてて食べてない。冷蔵庫に何かないかなと思ったら、母が買ってきたポケモンパンがあったのじゃ。
I forgot to chill the jelly today so I didn't eat it. When I looked to see if there was anything in the fridge, there was the Pokémon bread my mom bought, noja.

217
00:24:28,732 --> 00:24:33,232
今日は何のシールだったの？え、開ける、今！まだ開けてないの。Okay.
What sticker did I get today? Oh, I'll open it now! I haven't opened it yet. Okay.

218
00:24:34,532 --> 00:24:37,432
Let me see! 開けてみるね。
Let me see! I'll open it.

219
00:24:37,632 --> 00:24:43,732
Wait. じゃじゃじゃじゃじゃ〜！
Wait. Ta-da-da-da-da~!

220
00:24:43,832 --> 00:24:46,532
ラウドボーン！ホゲータの最終進化か？
Skeledirge! Is that Fuecoco's final evolution?

221
00:24:47,032 --> 00:24:49,232
ラウドボーンだったのじゃ。
It was Skeledirge, noja.

222
00:24:50,032 --> 00:24:52,532
午後2時、あ、午後2時なんだ。
2 PM... Oh, it's 2 PM for you.

223
00:24:53,632 --> 00:24:55,232
ラウドボーン。
Skeledirge.

224
00:24:56,232 --> 00:24:59,832
Mela, your English was so good during the Peak collab!

225
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:05,040
やった！Thank you のじゃー！やったのじゃー！
Yay! Thank you noja~! Yay noja~!

226
00:25:06,040 --> 00:25:07,840
ふりあさん？そうそうそうそう。
Furia-san? Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

227
00:25:08,140 --> 00:25:09,540
そう、確かに強い。
Yeah, he really is strong.

228
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:13,240
移動しながら聞いてる？やった！Thank you.
Are you listening while moving around? Yay! Thank you.

229
00:25:14,340 --> 00:25:20,040
うん。御三家、炎属性。そう、炎タイプなの！一緒なのじゃー！
Yeah. Starter Pokémon, fire attribute. Yeah, it's a Fire-type! It's the same as me noja~!

230
00:25:21,040 --> 00:25:23,040
確かにサラマンダーっぽいもんね。
True, it does look pretty salamander-like.

231
00:25:24,440 --> 00:25:26,540
よし！始めるか！
Alright! Shall we start?

232
00:25:27,540 --> 00:25:33,040
スタートイングリッシュ！リスニング... え、ルーシーと恐竜じゃって！おぬしら、ルーシーと恐竜...
Start English! Listening... Huh, it says "Lucy and the Dinosaurs"! You guys, "Lucy and the Dinosaurs"...

233
00:25:33,540 --> 00:25:38,240
ARKやってたから、あれなんじゃない？なん... 馴染みあるんじゃない？
Since we played ARK, wouldn't it be... like, familiar to us?

234
00:25:38,840 --> 00:25:41,040
ダイナソー。ダイナソー。
Dinosaurs. Dinosaurs.

235
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:47,540
行くよ、おぬしら。Let's start イングリッシュ配信のじゃー！
Here we go, everyone. Let's start the English stream noja~!

236
00:25:48,840 --> 00:25:50,440
Lucy and the Dinosaurs.

237
00:25:50,840 --> 00:25:54,040
Lucy and the Dinosaurs.

238
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:57,640
音量はいいかな？こんなもんで一旦始めるのじゃ？
Is the volume okay? Shall we get started around this level noja?

239
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:06,040
Lucy opens her apartment door.

240
00:26:06,340 --> 00:26:13,240
Lucy opens her apartment door. ルーシーはアパートのドアを開けました。
"Lucy opens her apartment door." Lucy opened the door to her apartment.

241
00:26:13,640 --> 00:26:15,040
Hello, Junior!

242
00:26:15,240 --> 00:26:17,040
Hello, Junior!

243
00:26:18,840 --> 00:26:21,840
わっぱ？よう、わっぱ。よう、わっぱ。
Kiddo? Yo, kiddo. Yo, kiddo.

244
00:26:22,040 --> 00:26:26,040
Hello! Can you help me write a report for school?

245
00:26:27,040 --> 00:26:32,440
ハロー！学校の宿題手伝ってくれない？
"Hello!" Could you help me with my school report?

246
00:26:33,140 --> 00:26:35,040
Oh, yes!

247
00:26:35,140 --> 00:26:39,840
いいわよ〜。ルーシーおばあさん... yes. いいわよ〜。
Sure~. Grandma Lucy... yes. Sure~.

248
00:26:40,040 --> 00:26:44,240
I'm a great writer. I have a very popular blog.

249
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:46,540
ブリティッシュイングリッシュじゃん。
That's British English right there.

250
00:26:47,640 --> 00:26:51,840
I'm a great writer. I have a very popular blog.

251
00:26:52,040 --> 00:27:00,040
ブログ... あ、私は超有名なブログを書く、すごいライターなのよ。
Blog... Ah, "I am a great writer who writes a super popular blog."

252
00:27:01,040 --> 00:27:07,540
ブログ書くの得意... えー、じゃあわしも戦えるかも。わしもかつてブログを書いていた時期があるから。
Good at writing blogs... Eh, then I might be able to relate too. Because I also used to write a blog back in the day.

253
00:27:08,040 --> 00:27:13,540
わしもかつてはてなブログとかをやっていた時期があるから。ちょっと気持ちわかるかも。
Because I also had a period where I ran a Hatena Blog and stuff. So I kind of understand how she feels.

254
00:27:16,040 --> 00:27:19,540
え、なんか... ブリティッシュじゃない？ブリティッシュっぽくない？発音。
Wait, isn't that... British? Doesn't the pronunciation sound British?

255
00:27:20,540 --> 00:27:22,540
Do you read my blog?

256
00:27:22,740 --> 00:27:24,840
Do you read my blog?

257
00:27:25,040 --> 00:27:28,540
子供に何読ませてんだ！子供に何読ませてんだ！
What are you making a kid read?! What are you making a kid read?!

258
00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:35,040
私のブログを読みたいかしらん？
"Would you like to read my blog?"

259
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:40,540
子供が読んでいいブログか？子供が読んでいいブログなのか？
Is that a blog suitable for children? Is it a blog that a kid should be reading?

260
00:27:42,540 --> 00:27:47,840
Do you read... あ、読破してますかのほう？読破してますかのほうなのかな？「読みたいかしら」じゃない？
"Do you read..." Ah, is it "Do you read it?" Is it asking if he reads it? Isn't it "Would you like to read it?"

261
00:27:49,040 --> 00:27:54,040
Do you read だから... 「読む？」「読むか？」「読む？」か。
Since it's "Do you read"... "Do you read it?", "Do you read it?", "Do you read it?"

262
00:27:55,040 --> 00:27:56,540
No.

263
00:27:56,640 --> 00:27:58,240
No. No.

264
00:27:58,340 --> 00:28:04,540
読んでいませんか... 読んだことある？読んだ？読んでない。「読むかい？」「読まない」。
"Are you not reading it?"... "Have you read it? Did you read it? No." "Do you read it?" "No, I don't."

265
00:28:05,040 --> 00:28:07,240
I have a lot of books.

266
00:28:07,540 --> 00:28:09,540
「たくさんの本を持っているわ。」
"I have a lot of books."

267
00:28:10,540 --> 00:28:13,540
Do you need my books for research?

268
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:18,040
Do you need my books for...

269
00:28:18,240 --> 00:28:27,540
あ、ブログ作戦失敗したから、「本いっぱいあるよ。本いっぱいあるけど、あの、宿題用に使う？」
Ah, since her blog plan failed, she's like, "I have lots of books. I have lots of books, so um, do you need them for your research?"

270
00:28:30,040 --> 00:28:32,240
I don't need your books.

271
00:28:33,040 --> 00:28:37,540
なんか振られまくりじゃない、ルーシー？ルーシー断られまくりじゃない？
Isn't Lucy getting rejected repeatedly? Isn't Lucy getting turned down over and over?

272
00:28:38,240 --> 00:28:43,040
I don't need your books.「別にいらんのじゃー。」
"I don't need your books." "I don't really need them noja~."

273
00:28:44,040 --> 00:28:45,540
What is your report about?

274
00:28:45,640 --> 00:28:49,040
「あなたは何についてのレポートを書いているの？」
"What are you writing your report about?"

275
00:28:50,040 --> 00:28:54,840
めらさんも本持ってる？そんなっぱい持ってないね。あんま本読まないのじゃ。
Do I have books? Not that many. I don't read books much noja.

276
00:28:55,240 --> 00:28:56,540
Dinosaurs.

277
00:28:56,840 --> 00:29:07,540
恐竜！あ、非常に... 非常に子供らしい。そうだよね。別に恐竜の本とかあんまルーシー読まなそうだもんね。恐竜の本が読みたいのじゃ。
Dinosaurs! Ah, very... very child-like. Right. After all, Lucy doesn't seem like the type to read dinosaur books much. He wants to read dinosaur books noja.

278
00:29:08,040 --> 00:29:10,540
Why do you want to talk to me?

279
00:29:10,840 --> 00:29:15,540
なんで... なんでわしにじゃあ話しかけたん？
Why... why did you talk to me then?

280
00:29:16,040 --> 00:29:18,040
Why do you want to talk to me?

281
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:24,540
「何について話してほしいか？ Why do you want to talk to me? Why... でも、なぜ？なぜだよ。」
"What do you want me to talk about? Why do you want to talk to me? Why... but why? Why indeed."

282
00:29:25,040 --> 00:29:28,040
Why do you want to talk to me?

283
00:29:29,840 --> 00:29:34,540
「なんで私に喋ってほしかったのか？」
"Why did you want to talk to me?"

284
00:29:36,540 --> 00:29:48,540
Why... Why do you want to talk to me? 「なんで私に話しかけたの？」「は？」「なんで恐竜なのに私に話しかけたの？なんで？」
Why... "Why do you want to talk to me?" "Why did you talk to me?" "Huh?" "Why did you talk to me if your report is about dinosaurs? Why?"

285
00:29:50,040 --> 00:29:51,540
Because you are very old.

286
00:29:51,640 --> 00:29:59,040
そういうことか！なんで... なんで恐竜について私に話...
So that's why! Why... why talk to me about dinosaurs...

287
00:30:00,096 --> 00:30:02,048
こけたんですか？って言ったら...
When she asked, "Did you fall?", then...

288
00:30:02,048 --> 00:30:06,548
Because you are very old.

289
00:30:06,848 --> 00:30:11,848
だって、だって... おばあちゃんだから恐竜見たかなと思って。
Because... Because she thought, "Since she's a grandma, maybe she's seen dinosaurs."

290
00:30:11,848 --> 00:30:16,048
そこまで、そこまでじゃないよ！rudeだよrude。
I'm not that... I'm not that old! That's rude, rude!

291
00:30:16,048 --> 00:30:18,848
失礼だよ。
That's disrespectful!

292
00:30:19,048 --> 00:30:21,548
なんだ？喧嘩売ってるのか？
What? Are you trying to pick a fight?

293
00:30:21,848 --> 00:30:27,048
いや、お年寄りだから恐竜も詳しいかなと思って...
"Well, since she's an elderly person, I thought she might be knowledgeable about dinosaurs..."

294
00:30:27,048 --> 00:30:30,848
おばあちゃんが子供の頃は恐竜いたんでしょ？
"When grandma was a child, there were dinosaurs, right?"

295
00:30:33,048 --> 00:30:37,548
めらが子供の時も恐竜いないけどな、別に。
Even when Mela was a child, there were no dinosaurs either.

296
00:30:37,548 --> 00:30:44,248
When I am youngでも... I was youngでも no dinosaursだけどな。
Even "when I am young"... even "I was young", there were no dinosaurs.

297
00:30:44,248 --> 00:30:46,048
わしもいない。
I didn't see any either.

298
00:30:46,848 --> 00:30:50,048
"Mela, can you tell us about dinosaurs?"

299
00:30:50,048 --> 00:30:55,248
No, I don't know! I never see dinosaurs!
No, I don't know! I've never seen dinosaurs!

300
00:30:55,548 --> 00:31:04,248
なんか恐竜の化石... 化石？なんだ、骨みたいなさ、博物館みたいなのはさ、見たけど。それ以外は知らんのじゃ。
I've seen dinosaur fossils... Fossils? Or like, bones in museums and such, but other than that, I don't know anything about them.

301
00:31:07,548 --> 00:31:14,548
Salamanders dinosaurs? No, サラマンダーはダイナソーじゃないよ。Salamanders is not dinosaurs.
"Salamanders are dinosaurs?" No, salamanders aren't dinosaurs. Salamanders are not dinosaurs.

302
00:31:15,048 --> 00:31:21,048
だって恐竜はいたじゃろ？サラマンダーの方が格上なのじゃ、どちらかというとね。
After all, dinosaurs actually existed, right? Salamanders are higher tier, so to speak.

303
00:31:21,048 --> 00:31:26,548
恐竜は火吹けないじゃろ？サラマンダーは火吹けるのじゃ。から違う。
Dinosaurs can't breathe fire, right? Salamanders CAN breathe fire! So they're different.

304
00:31:28,048 --> 00:31:30,248
鳥類じゃないよ、わし。
I'm not a bird!

305
00:31:30,548 --> 00:31:34,048
Yes, dinosaurs bird.
Yes, dinosaurs are birds.

306
00:31:34,048 --> 00:31:39,048
そう、恐竜は鳥じゃけど、わし鳥じゃないのじゃ。I am not birdなのじゃ。
Right, dinosaurs are birds, but I'm not a bird. I am not a bird!

307
00:31:40,048 --> 00:31:43,248
You know a lot of dinosaurs, right?

308
00:31:43,248 --> 00:31:47,048
たくさんの恐竜知ってるでしょ？ねえ？おばあちゃん。
"You know a lot of dinosaurs, right? Right, grandma?"

309
00:31:47,048 --> 00:31:52,548
Goodbye, Junior. Goodbye, Junior. Goodbye, Junior.

310
00:31:53,048 --> 00:32:00,048
バイバイ。バイバイ。もういい。もういいわ。「ありがとうございました」か。
Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Fine! I'm done. "Thank you very much," huh?

311
00:32:01,048 --> 00:32:04,548
なるほどね。なるほどね、かわいそうに。
I see. I see, poor thing.

312
00:32:05,048 --> 00:32:06,548
「たくさんの」a lot of
"A lot of" is "a lot of".

313
00:32:06,548 --> 00:32:08,548
「でしょ」right
"Right" is "right".

314
00:32:09,548 --> 00:32:12,048
「...は必要ありません」don't need
"...is not needed" is "don't need".

315
00:32:12,048 --> 00:32:14,048
「調べ物」research
"Things to research" is "research".

316
00:32:14,048 --> 00:32:15,848
「を開けます」opens
"Opens" is "opens".

317
00:32:16,048 --> 00:32:23,548
怒っていなくなっちゃった。わっぱ相手に... わっぱ相手に怒って、「もういい！もういいのじゃ、サラバじゃー！」って行っちゃった。
He got mad and left! At a kid... He got angry at a kid and went, "Enough! I've had enough, farewell!" and left.

318
00:32:24,548 --> 00:32:32,548
"Are salamanders and dinosaurs friends, Mela?" No, I don't... I never... I never see dinosaurs.

319
00:32:33,048 --> 00:32:37,048
Right. Rightのもっと使えるようになりたいのじゃ。
Right. I want to get better at using "right".

320
00:32:38,048 --> 00:32:42,048
かわいそうにおばあちゃん。そんな古くないよね。
Poor grandma. She's not that old, right?

321
00:32:42,248 --> 00:32:51,548
大人げない？ま、確かに大人げなくはある。大人げなくはあるが、ま、コンディションというものがね、人間はありますからね。イライラしやすい人というのもありますからね。
Childish? Well, true, it is a bit childish. It is a bit childish, but people have their bad condition days, you know. Some people get easily irritated, too.

322
00:32:52,048 --> 00:33:00,548
あの、普段は流せることがイライラしやすい日だと流せないみたいなことも大いに起こりうるので、そう言う日だったのでしょうかね。
Things that you can usually brush off, you might not be able to on days when you're easily irritated, so maybe it was just one of those days.

323
00:33:02,048 --> 00:33:10,048
次。ネクスト。わしは別にいいよ。わしはおばあちゃん扱いされてもいいよ。I'm...
Next! Next. I'm fine with it. I don't mind being treated like a grandma. I'm...

324
00:33:11,048 --> 00:33:23,048
Okay. If you guys say me grandma... でもいいよ。なぜならわしは本当におぬしらより長生きしてるからね。
Okay. "If you guys say me grandma"... that's fine too. Because I've actually lived much longer than you guys.

325
00:33:26,048 --> 00:33:39,048
いいよ、別にわしは。わし grandma... grandmaのじゃ。お子様扱い？ま、それもいいよ。好きなようにするといいよ。
I don't mind at all. Grandma... I'm Grandma-noja. Treated like a child? Well, that's fine too. You can do whatever you like.

326
00:33:41,048 --> 00:33:52,048
やす。grandmaのじゃ。やす、grandmaのじゃ。うん、やす。grandmaでも、あの、kidsでもいいよ。なんでもいいよ。
Yasu... Grandma-noja. Yasu, Grandma-noja. Yeah, yasu. Grandma, or kids, anything is fine. Whatever you like.

327
00:33:53,048 --> 00:34:05,048
好きに... 消費するといいのじゃ。お姫様でもいいよ。Princessでもいいよ。"Princess Mela is great, too."
Feel free to interpret it as you like. "Princess" is fine too. "Princess" is fine too. "Princess Mela is great, too."

328
00:34:06,548 --> 00:34:15,048
なんでもいいよ。好きなようにのじゃ。次行くか。
Anything is fine. As you like! Shall we move on to the next one?

329
00:34:17,048 --> 00:34:25,048
えっと、リスニングかな？リスニングなら... 一旦BGM切ってみるか？よし。
Um, listening maybe? For listening... Should I try turning off the BGM for a moment? Okay.

330
00:34:26,048 --> 00:34:31,048
スタート。リスニング？あ、ラジオ来た！Radio!
Start. Listening? Oh, radio came! Radio!

331
00:34:32,048 --> 00:34:35,048
フォルスタッフの人間入門... フォルスタッフの人間入門...
"Falstaff's Introduction to Humans..." "Falstaff's Introduction to Humans..."

332
00:34:35,048 --> 00:34:40,048
このコーナーでは、クマの僕が人間の世界のア不思議を究明していくよ。誰？フォルスタッフ？
"In this segment, I, a bear, will investigate the mysteries of the human world." Who's that? Falstaff?

333
00:34:44,048 --> 00:34:49,048
始める前に、次の単語を覚えておこう。うん。単語教えてくれるよ。
"Before we start, let's learn the following words." Mm. It's going to teach us vocabulary words.

334
00:34:50,048 --> 00:34:53,548
Mail. メイル。郵便かな？
"Mail." Mail. Is it post?

335
00:34:54,048 --> 00:34:56,548
Later. あとで。
"Later." Afterwards.

336
00:34:57,048 --> 00:35:00,048
Afternoon. 午後。
"Afternoon." Afternoon.

337
00:35:00,728 --> 00:35:01,406
Counter.

338
00:35:02,056 --> 00:35:03,556
カウンター
Counter...

339
00:35:04,056 --> 00:35:05,556
わしの天敵
My arch-nemesis.

340
00:35:06,906 --> 00:35:12,356
フォースタッフです。今回は頼みごとをしている二人を盗み聞きしていきたい。
This is Falstaff. Today, I'd like to eavesdrop on two people asking each other for favors.

341
00:35:12,856 --> 00:35:15,556
Hi John, could you water my plants?

342
00:35:15,556 --> 00:35:17,056
水やりやってくんない？
"Can you water them for me?"

343
00:35:15,556 --> 00:35:18,056
Of course! These flowers need water.

344
00:35:18,156 --> 00:35:20,956
Thank you. And can you check the mail every day?

345
00:35:19,456 --> 00:35:21,556
花は水必要としてる
"The flowers need water."

346
00:35:22,056 --> 00:35:24,056
郵便を毎日確認してくれる？
"Can you check the mail every day?"

347
00:35:25,056 --> 00:35:26,556
うん、頼んでいた。
Yeah, she was asking him.

348
00:35:28,056 --> 00:35:31,056
No problem. Can I put the mail on the counter?

349
00:35:29,056 --> 00:35:32,556
カウンターに郵便物置いていってもいいですか？
"Can I leave the mail on the counter?"

350
00:35:33,256 --> 00:35:35,056
Can I put the mail on the counter?

351
00:35:35,256 --> 00:35:41,556
Can I put the mail, the mail, on counter?

352
00:35:42,056 --> 00:35:42,856
にょじゃ。
Nyoja.

353
00:35:44,056 --> 00:35:46,856
Yes, please put it on the counter.

354
00:35:45,056 --> 00:35:47,056
いいよ、カウンターに置いてって。
"Sure, leave it on the counter."

355
00:35:47,056 --> 00:35:48,856
Don't worry, no problem.

356
00:35:48,556 --> 00:35:49,256
いいぞ。
Nice.

357
00:35:49,056 --> 00:35:51,056
Thank you John. Call me in the afternoon.

358
00:35:50,056 --> 00:35:51,256
ありがとう、John。
"Thanks, John."

359
00:35:51,556 --> 00:35:53,056
No problem. See you later.

360
00:35:52,556 --> 00:35:54,256
午後に電話してね。またね。
"Call me in the afternoon. See ya."

361
00:35:55,556 --> 00:35:57,056
彼女が彼に頼んだのは...
What she asked him to do was...

362
00:35:57,556 --> 00:35:59,856
午後に電話すること。
...to call her in the afternoon.

363
00:36:01,856 --> 00:36:12,556
他の人間に頼み事をして、お礼を言っている。自分でやったほうが早いだろうに。クマは自分で全部やるから、お礼なんて言う必要がない。
Asking another human for favors and thanking them... It'd be faster to do it yourself. Bears do everything themselves, so there's no need to say thanks.

364
00:36:06,056 --> 00:36:07,056
うん。
Yeah.

365
00:36:13,056 --> 00:36:16,556
今日のフォースタッフの盗み聞きもここまで。じゃあ、また。
That's all for Falstaff's eavesdropping today. Until next time.

366
00:36:13,256 --> 00:36:14,256
孤独！
Loneliness!

367
00:36:15,056 --> 00:36:17,556
孤独！孤独ベアー！
Lonely! Lonely bear!

368
00:36:18,056 --> 00:36:21,556
Alone, lonely... lonely bear.

369
00:36:22,056 --> 00:36:24,056
Lonely bear...

370
00:36:24,556 --> 00:36:29,056
0 lonely bear. だから...だから孤独なんじゃ。
0 lonely bear. That's... that's why he's lonely.

371
00:36:30,556 --> 00:36:42,556
The bear, Falstaff said, "I did all things with myself"...
The bear, Falstaff, said, "I do all things by myself"...

372
00:36:43,056 --> 00:36:47,856
一人でやったほうが早いって言ってた。「I did well...」
He said it's faster to do it alone. "I did well..."

373
00:36:49,056 --> 00:36:57,056
...than ask another people.
...than ask other people.

374
00:36:58,556 --> 00:37:00,056
って言ってたのじゃ。
...is what he said.

375
00:37:00,556 --> 00:37:05,556
うん、孤独だから言葉に協調性がなくてもいいってこと...いや、わしもね...
Yeah, because he's lonely, he doesn't need to be cooperative in how he speaks... Well, I used to be...

376
00:37:05,656 --> 00:37:16,556
わしもね、かつて孤独だったから言葉に協調性がなくても許されてきたんじゃけど、ホロライブに入ってしまった以上、協調性がないとちょっと嫌な思いをさせてしまうらしいから気をつけてたい気持ちあり。
Since I was also lonely in the past, I got away with not being cooperative with my words, but now that I've joined Hololive, if I'm not cooperative, it seems to cause unpleasant feelings for others, so I want to be mindful of that.

377
00:37:17,056 --> 00:37:22,556
孤独歴が長すぎて...孤独歴が長すぎて、コミュニケーションがガチで分からんのじゃ。
I've been lonely for way too long... My history of isolation is so long that I legitimately don't understand communication.

378
00:37:23,056 --> 00:37:26,056
あの、今わしはね、フォースタッフをね...
Um, right now I...

379
00:37:26,156 --> 00:37:29,056
フォースタッフをね、あの...
...talking about Falstaff, um...

380
00:37:30,056 --> 00:37:35,556
なんというか...わし、わしたちも...ちょっとそれは、それは言い過ぎやろって思っているけど...
How should I put it... I think saying "we're the same" might be an exaggeration, but...

381
00:37:35,656 --> 00:37:37,856
わしだってフォースタッフだった。
...I used to be Falstaff too.

382
00:37:38,256 --> 00:37:41,056
I was him.

383
00:37:41,556 --> 00:37:46,056
And also, I am him... a little.

384
00:37:48,556 --> 00:37:57,056
そうなんのじゃ。ガチで、コミュニケーション...人間が正常に行うコミュニケーションの経験値を積んでいない...
That's right. For real, I haven't gained the experience points for normal human communication...

385
00:37:57,556 --> 00:38:00,056
孤独サラマンダーじゃから...
...because I'm a lonely salamander...

386
00:38:01,056 --> 00:38:07,856
そう、お前は俺だって思うから、過去の自分って思うから、わしも頑張らなきゃな。
Yeah, seeing him feels like seeing my past self, so I have to try hard too.

387
00:38:08,556 --> 00:38:11,056
わしもこれ、頑張るね。
I'll do my best with this.

388
00:38:14,556 --> 00:38:20,556
協調性...なし。頑張る。自分でやった方が早いって思う...思う。
Cooperativeness... zero. I'll work hard. I think doing it myself is faster... I really do.

389
00:38:21,056 --> 00:38:23,556
自分でやった方が早いって思うけど...
Even though I think doing it myself is faster...

390
00:38:24,056 --> 00:38:27,556
協調性を身につけなきゃなと思う。
...I feel like I need to acquire social cooperation.

391
00:38:28,556 --> 00:38:32,056
See, you stupid, just like him, huh? Yes.

392
00:38:32,556 --> 00:38:33,856
そういうことなんのじゃ。
That's what it means.

393
00:38:37,556 --> 00:38:41,056
ソロマンダー...ソロマンダー...
Solomander... Solomander...

394
00:38:42,056 --> 00:38:46,856
いや、配信は一人じゃからできるんだけど、コラボになった瞬間...
I mean, I can stream fine on my own, but the moment it becomes a collaboration...

395
00:38:47,056 --> 00:38:51,556
コミュニケーションっていうのがね、新たに発生しちゃうから苦手なんのじゃ。
...communication newly enters the equation, which is why I'm bad at it.

396
00:38:52,556 --> 00:38:55,856
コミュニケーション苦手。1対1のコミュニケーションならまだしも...
I'm bad at communicating. One-on-one communication is one thing, but...

397
00:38:56,056 --> 00:38:58,856
3人以上のコミュニケーションが苦手なんのじゃ。
...I'm really bad at group conversations with three or more people.

398
00:38:59,056 --> 00:39:05,556
I'm not good at communicate with... more than three people...
I'm not good at communicating with... more than three people...

399
00:39:07,056 --> 00:39:14,556
なんか...会話にいつ入っていいか分かんなかったり...どういう発言が適切か分かんなくなったりするからね...
Like... I don't know when to jump into the conversation, or I get confused about what's appropriate to say...

400
00:39:15,556 --> 00:39:18,556
そう、冷静につっこんでるのはそれでかっていうのは...
Yeah, so when people ask why I react so deadpan or blunt...

401
00:39:19,056 --> 00:39:24,056
あの、素なのはあれが...つっこもうと思ってつっこんでないの。
...that's just my natural state... I'm not intentionally trying to play the straight man.

402
00:39:25,056 --> 00:39:26,556
素なの、わし。
That's just the real me.

403
00:39:27,056 --> 00:39:31,556
どうすればいいか分かんないの、人間と関わるとき。
I just don't know what to do when interacting with humans.

404
00:39:32,056 --> 00:39:35,056
あのコミュニケーションしか知らんのの。
That's the only way of communicating I know.

405
00:39:35,256 --> 00:39:36,056
じゃから...
So...

406
00:39:36,556 --> 00:39:40,856
I need some practice for communication.

407
